## 1. `NodeSummonMenu.tsx` — structure

File: `/Users/trang.doan/Desktop/code/discourse-graph/.claude/worktrees/eng-2478-add-a-type-filter-panel-to-the-obsidian-node/apps/obsidian/src/components/NodeSummonMenu.tsx`

Two things live here: a presentational React component `NodeSummonList`, and a non-React class `NodeSummonMenu` that owns the DOM container, keymap scope, listeners, and positioning. The class renders the component imperatively via `root.render(...)` on every state change — there is no React state anywhere; `activeIndex`, `query`, `groups` are class fields.

**React component** (lines 14–78):

```ts
type NodeSummonListProps = {
  groups: SummonMenuGroup[];
  activeIndex: number;
  onSelect: (index: number) => void;
  onHover: (index: number) => void;
};
```

Renders top-to-bottom:
- Empty state short-circuit (line 33–35): `<div className="suggestion-empty">No matching nodes</div>` — returns before the `.suggestion` wrapper at all.
- Otherwise a single `<div className="suggestion">` (line 39) containing, per group: a group header div (the type name + color dot), then one `suggestion-item` div per node.
- **There is no header row today.** The only header-ish element is the per-group type label (lines 42–58). A separator is applied to groups after the first via `groupIndex > 0 ? "border-modifier-border mt-1 border-0 border-t border-solid" : ""`.
- The flat `index` counter (line 37, `const itemIndex = index++`) is what maps group-nested nodes onto the single flat `activeIndex` space. Any new non-item row inserted into this tree must not participate in that counter.
- `activeRef` + `useEffect` (lines 27–31) scrolls the active item into view on `activeIndex` change: `activeRef.current?.scrollIntoView({ block: "nearest" })`.

**Styling approach**: mixed. Obsidian's built-in suggestion classes (`suggestion-container`, `suggestion`, `suggestion-item`, `is-selected`, `suggestion-empty`) plus Tailwind utilities (`flex items-center gap-2 px-3 pb-1 pt-2 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide`, `pl-7`, `invisible`) and Obsidian CSS vars through Tailwind arbitrary values (`bg-[var(--text-faint)]`, `border-modifier-border`, `text-muted`). Only one inline style is used, for the per-type color dot, because it's data-driven:

```ts
style={group.nodeType.color ? { backgroundColor: group.nodeType.color } : undefined}
```

Note AGENTS.md's "do not hardcode styles inline" rule — the existing exception is the dynamic color.

**Class fields** (lines 87–101): `view`, `trigger`, `triggerFrom`, `plugin`, `nodes: SearchableNode[]`, `query = ""`, `activeIndex = 0`, `groups: SummonMenuGroup[]`, `containerEl`, `root`, `scope`, `resizeObserver`, `cleanupListeners: (() => void)[]`, `closed`.

**Node loading** happens once per open (lines 108–111) — `new QueryEngine(plugin.app).getSearchableNodes(plugin.settings.nodeTypes)`; typing only re-ranks. So `this.nodes` is a stable superset a filter can re-derive from without re-querying the vault.

**Module singleton** (lines 324–335): `let activeMenu`, with `getActiveNodeSummonMenu()`, `openNodeSummonMenu(options)`, `closeNodeSummonMenu()`.

## 2. Fetching and grouping

File: `/Users/.../apps/obsidian/src/utils/nodeSummonResults.ts` (35 lines total).

The menu's only call site is `regroup()` (lines 266–272):

```ts
this.groups = groupNodesForSummonMenu({
  nodes: this.nodes,
  nodeTypes: this.plugin.settings.nodeTypes,
  query: this.query,
});
```

called from the constructor (line 192) and from `setQuery` (line 201), each followed by `render()`.

The util itself:

```ts
const MAX_NODES_PER_TYPE = 10;   // "Matches Roam's summoning menu."

export type SummonMenuGroup = { nodeType: DiscourseNode; nodes: RankedDiscourseNode[] };

export const groupNodesForSummonMenu = ({ nodes, nodeTypes, query }: {...}): SummonMenuGroup[] => {
  const ranked = rankDiscourseNodesByTitle({ candidates: nodes, query });
  return nodeTypes
    .map((nodeType) => ({
      nodeType,
      nodes: ranked.filter((n) => n.nodeTypeId === nodeType.id).slice(0, MAX_NODES_PER_TYPE),
    }))
    .filter((group) => group.nodes.length > 0);
};
```

Key points for planning: ranking is global over all candidates (one `rankDiscourseNodesByTitle({ candidates, query })` call), then partitioned per type and capped at 10 **after** ranking. Group order is `plugin.settings.nodeTypes` order, not score order. Empty groups are dropped — so the list of types visible in the menu is already a filtered subset of all configured types; a filter panel needs the full `nodeTypes` list from settings, not `groups`.

## 3. Keyboard handling

Scope is constructed as a child of the app scope and pushed (lines 125–153):

```ts
this.scope = new Scope(plugin.app.scope);
this.scope.register([], "ArrowDown", (evt) => evt.isComposing || this.move(1));
this.scope.register([], "ArrowUp",   (evt) => evt.isComposing || this.move(-1));
this.scope.register([], "Enter", (evt) => { ... });
this.scope.register([], "Escape", (evt) => { ... });
plugin.app.keymap.pushScope(this.scope);
```

Registered keys: exactly `ArrowDown`, `ArrowUp`, `Enter`, `Escape` — no Tab, no modifiers, no left/right. Returning `true` passes the key through to the editor; returning `false` consumes it.

Movement across groups (`move`, lines 234–242) is purely flat and wrapping:

```ts
const count = this.itemCount();
if (count === 0) return true;
this.activeIndex = (this.activeIndex + delta + count) % count;
```

with `itemCount()` = `this.groups.reduce((c, g) => c + g.nodes.length, 0)` (line 224). Selection resolves the index by flattening: `this.groups.flatMap((group) => group.nodes)[index]` (line 245). `setQuery` resets `activeIndex = 0` (line 200).

Enter (lines 138–147): composing → pass through; `closeIfEditorBlurred()` → pass through; zero items → `close()` and pass through; otherwise `select(this.activeIndex)` and consume.

Escape (lines 148–152): composing or editor-blurred → pass through; otherwise `close()` and consume. There is no intermediate state — Escape always destroys the whole menu.

**Where a focusable sub-panel conflicts.** Three places:

1. The editor keeps DOM focus the whole time — enforced at lines 118–121:
   ```ts
   this.containerEl.addEventListener("mousedown", (event) => event.preventDefault());
   ```
   Every mousedown anywhere in the container is prevented, so nothing inside the menu can ever take focus. A focusable panel control would need this blanket preventDefault narrowed.
2. `closeIfEditorBlurred()` (lines 228–232) closes the menu on the next Arrow/Enter/Escape whenever `this.view.hasFocus` is false. If a panel control took focus, the very next key press would close the menu.
3. The pushed Scope is app-global while open, so ArrowUp/Down/Enter/Escape are intercepted before any panel element's own handlers. Panel navigation would have to be routed through these same four registrations (a mode flag on the class), or the Scope would have to conditionally return `true`. `keyboardHints.ts` exports `activateOnKey` (below), which is the existing pattern for "div rows that need Enter/Space" — but it is a React `onKeyDown` handler, which the pushed Scope would preempt for Enter.

## 4. Positioning and sizing

`reposition()` (lines 290–321) runs inside a CodeMirror measure phase:

```ts
this.view.requestMeasure({
  read: (view) => view.coordsAtPos(this.triggerFrom),
  write: (coords) => { ... },
});
```

The write phase:
- Closes the menu if `coords` is null or the trigger scrolled outside `this.view.scrollDOM.getBoundingClientRect()`.
- Reads `const { width, height } = this.containerEl.getBoundingClientRect()` — measured from the live element, so the menu's rendered size drives placement.
- Clamps `left` within `VIEWPORT_MARGIN = 8` (line 12) of both window edges.
- Flip-up logic: `openUpward = spaceBelow < height + VIEWPORT_MARGIN && coords.top > spaceBelow`; top is `coords.top - height - 4` when flipping, else `coords.bottom + 4`.
- Removes `invisible` on first placement (line 318) — container is created with `invisible` (line 116).

Repositioning triggers: constructor (line 194), `scrollDOM` scroll listener (lines 181–185), and a `ResizeObserver` on the container itself using the editor's own window (lines 187–190: `new win.ResizeObserver(() => this.reposition())`). The ResizeObserver means a panel expanding inside the menu automatically re-runs placement, including the flip-up decision — relevant because an expanding panel could make the menu taller than the space below the cursor mid-interaction.

**Max-height/overflow is not in this file.** The comment at line 122 says the sizing cap lives in CSS: *"Mounted directly so `.suggestion` is the flex child that scrolls inside the capped container."* That cap is on `.dg-node-summon-menu` / `.suggestion-container` in the plugin's stylesheet (I wasn't scoped to read it). The structural consequence is concrete though: the container is a flex column whose single flex child `.suggestion` is the scroller. A header row or filter panel added as a **sibling** of `.suggestion` inside `containerEl` would be pinned and non-scrolling; added **inside** `.suggestion` it would scroll away with the list.

## 5. Closing on outside click / blur

Outside-click (lines 155–162):

```ts
const closeIfOutside = (event: MouseEvent): void => {
  if (this.containerEl.contains(event.target as Node)) return;
  this.close();
};
doc.addEventListener("mousedown", closeIfOutside, true);
this.cleanupListeners.push(() => doc.removeEventListener("mousedown", closeIfOutside, true));
```

Capture-phase on the document, with a `containerEl.contains` guard. **Anything rendered inside `containerEl` already survives outside-click close** — so a filter panel mounted within the menu's own container needs no change here. A panel rendered in a portal, a separate `body` div, or an Obsidian `Menu`/popover would fall outside `containerEl` and close the menu on click; that's the case requiring a change.

The companion mousedown preventDefault (lines 118–121) means clicks in the panel won't blur the editor either — but it also means panel controls can't receive focus (see §3).

Other close paths:
- `closeIfEditorBlurred()` on any registered key when `!this.view.hasFocus`.
- Workspace events (lines 164–178): `file-open` when the opened file's path differs from the editor's, and `active-leaf-change` when `!leaf?.view.containerEl.contains(view.dom)`. Note these use `workspace.on(...)` + `workspace.offref(...)` directly rather than `registerEvent`, with cleanup through `cleanupListeners`.
- `reposition()` closing when the trigger scrolls out of the scroller.
- `nodeSummonExtension.ts` calling `closeNodeSummonMenu()` when the trigger text is destroyed, the cursor leaves the query, or the query starts with whitespace (lines 47–57 there).

`close()` (lines 205–221) is idempotent via `this.closed`, pops the scope, disconnects the observer, defers `root.unmount()` to a `setTimeout(…, 0)` on the editor's window (because unmounting during React event handling warns), removes `containerEl`, and nulls `activeMenu`.

## 6. Existing unit tests

File: `/Users/.../apps/obsidian/src/utils/__tests__/nodeSummonResults.test.ts` — four tests, all on `groupNodesForSummonMenu`:

1. `"groups nodes under their type in settings order and hides empty types"` — passes `[CLAIM, EVIDENCE, QUESTION]` with nodes only for claim/evidence; asserts output order follows settings order and `Question` is absent.
2. `"keeps only the top 10 results for each type"` — 12 claims named `L..A`; asserts the 10 best (`A`–`J`) survive.
3. `"narrows each type to matching titles, best match first"` — query `"actin"`; asserts `["Actin cap", "Branched actin"]` and that the Evidence group disappears.
4. `"returns no groups when nothing matches"` — expects `[]`.

Scaffolding worth reusing: `vi.mock("obsidian", ...)` replaces `prepareFuzzySearch` with a deterministic stand-in scorer (earlier substring index = higher score, lines 8–16); `nodeType(id, name)` builds a `DiscourseNode` with no `color` field set; `node(title, nodeTypeId)` builds a `SearchableNode` from a real `TFile`; `summarize(groups)` flattens to `{ type, titles }[]` for assertions. There are **no tests for `NodeSummonMenu.tsx` itself** anywhere — the component/class is untested, consistent with AGENTS.md ("logic that needs a live vault, editor, or workspace is not covered here — extract the decision into a util and test that"). A filter's selection logic would belong in a util to be testable.
